              "id": "beat-1",
              "text": "Sunlight looks white, but it actually contains a broad spectrum of wavelengths, from red to violet. When this light enters Earth's atmosphere, it interacts with gas molecules, primarily nitrogen and oxygen."
            },
            {
              "id": "beat-2",
              "text": "Because these gas molecules are much smaller than the wavelengths of visible light, they scatter the incoming sunlight in all directions through a process called Rayleigh scattering. Under this process, shorter wavelengths of light, like blue and violet, are scattered far more efficiently than longer wavelengths like red and orange."
            },
            {
              "id": "beat-3",
              "text": "But why isn't the sky violet, since violet has the shortest wavelength and scatters most intensely? It turns out sunlight contains less violet light than blue to begin with, and violet light undergoes greater atmospheric attenuation, filtering it out before it reaches our eyes."
            },
            {
              "id": "beat-4",
              "text": "Furthermore, our eyes are biologically much more sensitive to blue than violet. When scattered light stimulates our color receptors, our brain interprets the mix as an unsaturated, pale blue. This blue is softened even further toward white near the horizon, where larger dust and water vapor particles scatter all colors uniformly."
            }
          ],
